task.body:
`spec_id_for_fork` in `native/onchain_evm/src/lib.rs:98-102` hard-rejects every chain id except Ethereum mainnet:

```rust
fn spec_id_for_fork(chain_id: u64, block_number: u64) -> Result<SpecId, EvmError> {
    if chain_id != ETHEREUM_MAINNET_CHAIN_ID {
        return Err(EvmError::ForkError(format!(
            "unsupported chain id {chain_id}: hardfork schedule is known only for Ethereum mainnet (chain id {ETHEREUM_MAINNET_CHAIN_ID})"
        )));
    }
    ...
}
```

`lib/onchain/evm.ex:34-40` documents the same constraint in the `Onchain.EVM` moduledoc's "Fork revision" section: "RPC endpoints whose chain id is not Ethereum mainnet (`1`) return `{:error, {:fork_error, _}}` rather than silently executing under mainnet rules." Both are working as designed today — this is not a bug, it is a deliberately narrow feature that now blocks real consumers.

This is the direct extension of Task 60 (`Derive the EVM revision from the forked block instead of always executing under one modern SpecId`), which built the mainnet-only `MAINNET_HARDFORK_BLOCKS` schedule and explicitly scoped "supporting chains beyond those the module already targets" out. That scope boundary is what this task now needs to cross.

**Concrete blocking case:** `onchain_aerodrome` targets Aerodrome Finance on Base (chain id 8453). Its intended test strategy is revm fork tests against a pinned Base block plus a `simulate/2` path for validating writes before broadcast — both impossible today, since any fork attempt against a Base RPC returns `{:error, {:fork_error, _}}`. It has fallen back to golden fixtures plus pinned-block `eth_call`, which cannot validate write paths at all. The same blocker hits every OP-Stack chain (Optimism, chain id 10; Base, chain id 8453) and Arbitrum.

**Two shapes to evaluate, not presuppose:**
1. Add an OP-Stack/Base hardfork-schedule table (mirroring `MAINNET_HARDFORK_BLOCKS`) so `spec_id_for_fork` resolves known L2 chain ids against their own activation history.
2. A caller-supplied `:spec_id` option that bypasses the built-in schedule lookup entirely, putting correctness burden on the caller. Likely far smaller and unblocks every chain at once, but is a weaker long-term guarantee for chains this library wants to support first-class.

Pick with evidence — option 2 may be the right immediate unblock even if option 1 (or both together) is the correct long-term answer for chains this library supports directly.
